INT. DEEP-SPACE SURVEY CAPSULE - SLEEP CYCLE

A tube the size of a van. Cramped. Every surface a panel or a cabinet. A single porthole shows a pinhole star field, motionless.

TAMARA OSEI, 40s, floats in a sleeping bag strapped to the wall. Her eyes are open. She hasn't slept.

She unzips. Drifts to the console. Types a command with one finger.

A soft chime. A woman's recorded voice, calm, from the speaker.

TAMARA (V.O.)
(recording)
Day four hundred and twelve. All systems nominal. Crew morale: fine.

She stops the playback. Her thumb stays on the button.

She opens a locker. Takes out a small paper photograph, creased. Holds it to the light. Puts it back without looking.

She types again. Another chime.

TAMARA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recording)
Day four hundred and thirteen. All systems nominal. Crew morale: fine.

She plays it again. And again. Same words, same tone, same breath in the same place.

She sets the recorder down. It spins slowly, tumbling in the air.

She presses her palm flat to the porthole. The glass is cold. Her breath fogs it.

She wipes it with her sleeve. The stars come back.

She presses record.

TAMARA
Day four hundred and fourteen.

A long pause.

TAMARA (CONT'D)
All systems nominal.

She doesn't say the rest. She turns the recorder off and floats there, hand still against the glass.
